select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 2, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.0", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.0 Overview", "body": "Bank for International Settlements (2025). OTC derivatives statistics at end-June 2025. Basel: BIS.\nBlack, F., and Scholes, M. (1973). The pricing of options and corporate liabilities. Journal of Political Economy, 81(3), 637–654.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 1.\nMcDonald, R. L. (2013). Derivatives Markets (3rd ed.). Pearson. Chapters 1–2.\nMerton, R. C. (1973). Theory of rational option pricing. Bell Journal of Economics and Management Science, 4(1), 141–183."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.0", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow much cash must Northwind hold in reserve to keep its hedge?\nNorthwind Components hedges its purchase of 500,000 pounds of copper, due in six months, with a long position in 20 copper futures contracts at $4.10 per pound. The exchange requires a deposit of $120,000 when the position is opened. Losses on the position are collected in cash each day, and if the deposit is depleted Northwind must replenish it at once or lose the position. Select the cash reserve, beyond the initial deposit, that the treasurer should set aside for this purpose. Any balance above the required deposit is returned to the reserve each day."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.0#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.0", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.0 Decide first", "body": "The simulation assumes daily price changes with a standard deviation of 1.4 percent and no trend. The hedge is sound in every simulated path: a loss on the futures is matched by a lower cost of copper at the end of the six months. The difficulty is one of timing. The futures losses must be paid in cash as they occur, while the offsetting saving arrives only when the copper is bought. A hedger who cannot meet a margin call loses the hedge at the moment it is most needed. This chapter explains the mechanism that produces these cash demands and the reason exchanges impose it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.1", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.1", "heading": "The specification of a futures contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.1 The specification of a futures contract", "body": "Chapter 1 defined a futures contract as a standardized agreement, traded on an exchange, to buy or sell an asset at a future date for a price agreed today. Because the two parties to a futures trade do not negotiate with each other, the exchange must state in advance every term other than the price. This statement is the contract specification. It has five principal elements.\nThe underlying asset. For a financial asset such as a currency or a stock index, the asset is unambiguous. For a commodity, quality varies, and the exchange specifies the grades that may be delivered and any price adjustment for each. The copper contract of the COMEX division of CME Group, for example, calls for Grade 1 electrolytic copper cathodes.\nThe contract size. The contract size is the quantity of the asset to be delivered under one contract. It determines the notional value of a contract, equal to the futures price multiplied by the contract size. A copper futures price of $4.10 per pound and a contract size of 25,000 pounds give a notional value of \\(4.10\\times25{,}000=\\$102{,}500\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.1", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.1", "heading": "The specification of a futures contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.1 The specification of a futures contract", "body": "The delivery arrangements. The exchange specifies where and how delivery may be made. For many commodities the party with the short position may choose among several approved locations.\nThe delivery months. A contract is identified by its delivery month, such as the December copper contract. The exchange specifies which months are listed, the period within the month during which delivery may occur, and the last day on which the contract trades.\nThe price quotation and limits. The exchange specifies the units in which the price is quoted and the tick size, the smallest permitted price change. Multiplying the tick size by the contract size gives the tick value, the change in the value of one contract when the price moves by one tick. Many exchanges also set daily price limits, which halt or restrict trading when the price moves by more than a stated amount in one day, and position limits, which cap the number of contracts a single speculator may hold.\nTable 2.1 Specifications of five actively traded CME Group futures contracts\nContractContract sizePrice quotationTick sizeTick valueSettlement\nCopper (COMEX)25,000 poundsDollars per pound$0.0005$12.50Physical delivery"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.1#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.1", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.1", "heading": "The specification of a futures contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.1 The specification of a futures contract", "body": "Gold (COMEX)100 troy ouncesDollars per ounce$0.10$10.00Physical delivery\nWTI crude oil (NYMEX)1,000 barrelsDollars per barrel$0.01$10.00Physical delivery\nE-mini S&P 500$50 × indexIndex points0.25 point$12.50Cash\nEuro FX€125,000Dollars per euro$0.00005$6.25Physical delivery\nThe practical use of the specification is the conversion of a price change into a monetary gain or loss:\n\\[ \\text{Gain on a long position} = (\\text{change in futures price}) \\times (\\text{contract size}) \\times (\\text{number of contracts}). \\]\nThe gain on a short position is the negative of this amount. If copper futures rise from $4.10 to $4.16, a long position of 20 contracts gains \\(0.06\\times25{,}000\\times20=\\$30{,}000\\).\nConcept check 2.1\nA trader is short 8 E-mini S&P 500 contracts. The futures price rises from 5,812.50 to 5,830.00. What is the trader's gain or loss?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.2", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.2", "heading": "Convergence of the futures price to the spot price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.2 Convergence of the futures price to the spot price", "body": "As the delivery period of a futures contract approaches, the futures price converges to the spot price of the underlying asset, and during the delivery period the two are equal or very nearly so. The reason is an arbitrage argument of the kind introduced in Section 1.7.\nSuppose that during the delivery period the futures price were above the spot price. A trader could sell a futures contract, buy the asset in the spot market, and make delivery, receiving the futures price for an asset bought at the lower spot price. The profit would be immediate and without risk. Traders exploiting it would sell futures and buy the asset until the difference disappeared. Suppose instead that the futures price were below the spot price. Any firm that intended to acquire the asset would buy futures and take delivery in preference to buying in the spot market, and this demand would raise the futures price. In either case the difference cannot persist.\nThe difference between the spot price and the futures price at any time is the basis:\n\\[ \\text{Basis} = \\text{Spot price} - \\text{Futures price}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.2", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.2", "heading": "Convergence of the futures price to the spot price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.2 Convergence of the futures price to the spot price", "body": "Convergence is the statement that the basis approaches zero at maturity. Before maturity the basis may be positive or negative and it changes from day to day; Chapter 4 determines its theoretical value, and Chapter 5 examines the risk it creates for hedgers."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "Two parties who agree to trade at a future date each bear the risk that the other will not perform. A party whose position has lost value has an incentive to default, and may lack the means to pay. The exchange removes this risk by two devices used together: margin, and the daily settlement of gains and losses.\nA trader who enters a futures position must deposit funds with a broker in a margin account. The amount required when the position is opened is the initial margin. It is a performance deposit and not a payment toward the asset; it remains the property of the trader. At the end of each trading day the exchange announces a settlement price for the contract, and every margin account is adjusted to reflect the day's gain or loss. This procedure is daily settlement, also called marking to market. If the futures price rose during the day, the accounts of traders with long positions are credited, and the accounts of traders with short positions are debited by the same total amount; if the price fell, the transfers run the other way."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "To ensure that the balance never becomes negative, the exchange sets a maintenance margin, somewhat below the initial margin. If the balance falls below the maintenance margin, the trader receives a margin call and must deposit additional funds to restore the balance to the initial margin level, and not merely to the maintenance level. The amount deposited in response to a margin call is the variation margin. A trader who does not meet the call has the position closed by the broker. A trader may withdraw any balance in excess of the initial margin."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Key terms", "kind": "term", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Key terms", "body": "Key terms: Initial margin: the deposit required when a position is opened. Maintenance margin: the balance below which a margin call is issued. Margin call: a demand for additional funds. Variation margin: the funds deposited to return the balance to the initial margin. Settlement price: the price, set by the exchange at the close of each day, at which gains and losses are computed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "example", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "Worked example 2.1 — Northwind's margin account\nNorthwind takes a long position in 20 copper futures at $4.10. The initial margin is $6,000 per contract and the maintenance margin is $4,500 per contract. Settlement prices on the next five days are $4.08, $4.05, $4.03, $4.06, and $4.12. Construct the record of the margin account.\n1\nSet-up. The position covers \\(20\\times25{,}000=500{,}000\\) pounds. Initial margin: \\(20\\times6{,}000=\\$120{,}000\\). Maintenance margin: \\(20\\times4{,}500=\\$90{,}000\\).\n2\nDay 1. The price falls by $0.02. Loss: \\(0.02\\times500{,}000=\\$10{,}000\\). Balance: $110,000, which is above $90,000; no call.\n3\nDay 2. The price falls by $0.03. Loss: $15,000. Balance: $95,000; no call.\n4\nDay 3. The price falls by $0.02. Loss: $10,000. Balance: $85,000, which is below $90,000. Margin call: \\(120{,}000-85{,}000=\\$35{,}000\\). Balance after the deposit: $120,000.\n5\nDays 4 and 5. The price rises by $0.03 and then by $0.06. Gains: $15,000 and $30,000. Balances: $135,000 and $165,000. Northwind may withdraw up to $45,000.\n6"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "example", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "Check. The futures price rose from $4.10 to $4.12 over the five days, a cumulative gain of \\(0.02\\times500{,}000=\\$10{,}000\\). Final balance: \\(120{,}000+35{,}000+10{,}000=\\$165{,}000\\). The total gain depends only on the first and last prices; the path of prices determines the margin calls."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 2.1 — The margin account, day by day\nDaily settlement of 20 copper futures\nThe table and figure reproduce Worked example 2.1. Change the side of the position, or generate a new ten-day path of settlement prices, and confirm each entry by hand: the daily gain, the test against the maintenance margin, and the size of each margin call."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "warning", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "Common error\nA margin call restores the balance to the initial margin, not to the maintenance margin. In Worked example 2.1 the call on Day 3 is $35,000, the amount that returns the balance from $85,000 to $120,000. A deposit of $5,000, which would raise the balance only to the maintenance level of $90,000, does not satisfy the call."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.3#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.3", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.3", "heading": "Margin accounts and daily settlement", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.3 Margin accounts and daily settlement", "body": "Margin levels are set by the exchange with reference to the variability of the futures price: the more variable the price, the higher the margin. The initial margin is typically chosen to cover the loss on a position over one or two days with a high degree of confidence, and it is revised when market conditions change. Margin is commonly a small fraction of notional value, here \\(6{,}000/102{,}500\\), or about 5.9 percent, and this is the source of the leverage in futures positions discussed in Chapter 1. Most brokers pay interest on cash margin balances, and securities such as Treasury bills are usually accepted in place of cash at a discount to their market value, known as a haircut."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.4", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.4", "heading": "The clearinghouse and central clearing", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.4 The clearinghouse and central clearing", "body": "The clearinghouse of an exchange stands between the two parties to every trade. Once a trade is matched, the clearinghouse becomes the buyer to the seller and the seller to the buyer, so that each party's contract is with the clearinghouse alone. Only clearing members, which are firms that meet the capital requirements of the clearinghouse, deal with it directly; a broker that is not a clearing member must clear its trades through a firm that is. The margin that a clearing member posts with the clearinghouse is clearing margin. It is settled daily in the same manner as a customer's account, with one difference: there is no maintenance level, and the balance is restored to the required amount every day."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.4", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.4", "heading": "The clearinghouse and central clearing", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.4 The clearinghouse and central clearing", "body": "The clearinghouse is protected by a sequence of resources, applied in order if a member defaults: the margin of the defaulting member; that member's contribution to a default fund (or guaranty fund) to which all members contribute; a portion of the clearinghouse's own capital; and the default fund contributions of the surviving members. This ordered sequence is the default waterfall. Its design gives every member an interest in the soundness of the others, and losses to customers from the failure of a major clearinghouse have been rare."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.4", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.4", "heading": "Central clearing in over-the-counter markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.4 Central clearing in over-the-counter markets", "body": "Before the financial crisis of 2007–2009, most OTC derivatives were cleared bilaterally: the two parties dealt directly with each other under a master agreement, usually one published by the International Swaps and Derivatives Association (ISDA), with or without an annex requiring collateral. The failure of Lehman Brothers in September 2008, with a very large number of OTC contracts outstanding, showed how difficult it was to assess exposures in a bilateral market.\nRegulation since then, including the Dodd–Frank Act in the United States and the European Market Infrastructure Regulation in the European Union, requires standardized OTC derivatives between financial institutions to be cleared through a central counterparty (CCP). A CCP performs the function of an exchange clearinghouse: it becomes the counterparty to both sides, collects initial margin, and settles variation margin daily. Contracts that remain bilateral are subject to rules that require most financial institutions to exchange both initial and variation margin. The OTC market has therefore moved substantially toward the arrangements long used for futures."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.5", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.5", "heading": "Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.5 Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "body": "The great majority of futures contracts do not lead to delivery. A trader leaves the market by closing out the position, that is, by entering the opposite trade in the same contract. A trader who is long 20 December copper contracts sells 20 December copper contracts; the clearinghouse nets the two positions to zero, and the trader's total gain or loss is the difference between the price at which the contracts were sold and the price at which they were bought. A hedger such as Northwind normally closes out its futures shortly before the delivery month and purchases copper from its usual supplier; convergence ensures that the gain or loss on the futures offsets the change in the spot price it pays."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.5", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.5", "heading": "Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.5 Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "body": "If a position is held to maturity, it is settled in one of two ways. Under physical delivery, the party with the short position delivers the asset and receives the settlement price; the exchange specifies the procedure, which begins when the short issues a notice of intention to deliver. Under cash settlement, used where delivery of the underlying is impractical, as with a stock index, all open positions are marked to a final settlement price based on the spot value of the underlying, and no asset changes hands.\nTwo statistics describe the activity in a contract. Trading volume is the number of contracts traded during a day. Open interest is the number of contracts outstanding at a point in time, which equals the total of all long positions and, equivalently, the total of all short positions. A trade changes open interest according to the positions of the two parties before it.\nTable 2.2 Effect of one trade of one contract on open interest\nBuyerSellerChange in open interest\nOpens a new long positionOpens a new short positionIncreases by one\nOpens a new long positionCloses an existing long positionNo change\nCloses an existing short positionOpens a new short positionNo change"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.5", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.5", "heading": "Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.5 Closing out, delivery, and open interest", "body": "Closes an existing short positionCloses an existing long positionDecreases by one\nConcept check 2.2\nOn a given day 3,000 contracts trade. In 1,800 of these trades both parties open new positions; in 700 both parties close existing positions; in the remaining 500 one party opens and the other closes. By how much does open interest change?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.6#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.6", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.6", "heading": "Orders and market participants", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.6 Orders and market participants", "body": "A trader communicates an intention to trade through an order. A market order is an instruction to trade at once at the best price available. A limit order specifies a price and may be executed only at that price or one more favorable to the trader; it guarantees the price but not execution. A stop order, or stop-loss order, becomes a market order when the price reaches a specified level that is less favorable than the current price, and is used to limit the loss on an existing position. Orders are valid for the trading day unless stated otherwise; an order that remains in force until executed or cancelled is a good-till-cancelled order.\nNearly all futures trading is now electronic: orders are entered into the exchange's matching system, which pairs buyers with sellers by price and then by time of entry. In reports published by regulators such as the Commodity Futures Trading Commission in the United States, participants are classified as commercial traders, who use futures in connection with a business in the underlying asset and are predominantly hedgers, and non-commercial traders, who are predominantly speculators."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.7#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.7", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.7", "heading": "Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.7 Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "body": "Table 2.3 Forward and futures contracts\nFeatureForwardFutures\nMarketOver the counterExchange\nTermsNegotiated; any quantity and dateStandardized size, grade, and delivery months\nSettlement of gains and lossesOnce, at maturity (unless collateral is exchanged)Daily, through the margin account\nCounterpartyThe other party, or a CCP if clearedThe clearinghouse\nUsual terminationDelivery or final cash settlementClosing out before maturity\nDelivery dateA single specified dateUsually a range of dates in the delivery month"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.7", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.7", "heading": "Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.7 Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "body": "The total gain on a futures position held to maturity equals that on a forward with the same price and size. What differs is its timing. Had Northwind entered a forward at $4.10 in place of the futures in Worked example 2.1, it would have paid and received nothing during the five days; the futures position required a payment of $35,000 on Day 3 and stood $45,000 above the required balance on Day 5. The opening exercise of this chapter measured the consequence: a futures hedge must be supported by liquid funds that a forward hedge, in the absence of a collateral agreement, does not require. In exchange, the futures position carries almost no credit risk and can be closed at any time at a market price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.7#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.7", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.7", "heading": "Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.7 Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "body": "★ Optional note: are forward and futures prices equal? When interest rates are constant, the forward price and the futures price for the same asset and maturity are equal. When interest rates vary unpredictably, they can differ. Suppose that the price of the underlying asset tends to rise when interest rates rise. A long futures position then receives its daily gains when rates are high, so that the gains can be invested at high rates, and pays its daily losses when rates are low, so that they can be financed cheaply. A long forward has no such advantage, because it settles only at maturity. The futures contract is therefore the more attractive of the two, and its price is slightly higher. When the correlation is negative, the futures price is slightly lower. For contracts with maturities of a few months the difference is usually negligible, and it is ignored in this book except in the treatment of interest rate futures in Chapter 6.\nConcept check 2.3\nA company can hedge a purchase due in nine months with either a forward contract from its bank or exchange-traded futures. Which consideration favors the forward?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.7#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.7", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.7", "heading": "Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "kind": "notes", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.7 Forward contracts and futures contracts compared", "body": "Historical note\nIn the nineteenth-century grain markets of Chicago, contracts for future delivery were settled between the parties themselves, and defaults followed every sharp movement in prices. The Chicago Board of Trade responded in stages: it standardized contract terms in 1865 and required margin deposits in the same period, and in 1925 it established the Board of Trade Clearing Corporation, which took the opposite side of every trade.\nThe episode of Metallgesellschaft in 1993, listed in Table 1.3, is the standard illustration of the liquidity demands of daily settlement. A subsidiary had sold long-term fixed-price contracts to supply petroleum products and hedged them with short-dated oil futures that were renewed as they matured. When oil prices fell, the futures produced immediate losses that had to be paid in cash, while the offsetting gains on the supply contracts would be received only over years. The parent company closed the positions at a loss of approximately $1.3 billion."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.summary#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.summary", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.summary Summary", "body": "A futures contract specification fixes the asset, the contract size, the delivery arrangements and months, and the manner of price quotation. The gain on a long position is the price change multiplied by the contract size and the number of contracts.\nArbitrage between the spot and futures markets forces the futures price to converge to the spot price at maturity; the basis, spot minus futures, approaches zero.\nGains and losses are settled daily through margin accounts. A balance below the maintenance margin produces a margin call for the amount that restores the initial margin.\nThe clearinghouse is the counterparty to every trade and is protected by margin and by a default waterfall. Standardized OTC derivatives are now cleared through central counterparties in a similar way.\nMost futures positions are closed out before maturity; the remainder are settled by physical delivery or in cash. Open interest counts the contracts outstanding; volume counts the contracts traded.\nA futures position and a forward position produce the same total gain but different cash flows over time. Daily settlement removes credit risk and creates a need for liquidity."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.exercises Exercises", "body": "2.1 A trader buys 5 gold futures contracts at $2,640.00 per ounce and closes the position at $2,611.50. Using Table 2.1, compute the gain or loss.\nPrice change: \\(2{,}611.50-2{,}640.00=-28.50\\) per ounce. Loss: \\(28.50\\times100\\times5=\\$14{,}250\\).\n2.2 A trader takes a short position in 4 crude oil futures at $72.00 per barrel. The initial margin is $7,000 per contract and the maintenance margin is $5,500 per contract. At what futures price does the trader receive a margin call? How much could be withdrawn if the price fell to $70.00?\nInitial margin $28,000; maintenance $22,000. A call occurs when losses exceed $6,000. A short position loses when the price rises: \\(6{,}000/(4\\times1{,}000)=\\$1.50\\) per barrel, so a call occurs if the settlement price is above $73.50. At $70.00 the gain is \\(2.00\\times4{,}000=\\$8{,}000\\); the balance is $36,000 and $8,000 may be withdrawn.\n2.3 A trader is long 10 Euro FX futures entered at 1.1000. Initial margin is $2,800 per contract and maintenance margin $2,500 per contract. Settlement prices on the next four days are 1.0990, 1.0965, 1.0950, and 1.1010. Construct the margin account."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.exercises Exercises", "body": "The position covers €1,250,000; initial margin $28,000; maintenance $25,000. Day 1: \\(-0.0010\\times1{,}250{,}000=-\\$1{,}250\\); balance $26,750. Day 2: \\(-0.0025\\times1{,}250{,}000=-\\$3{,}125\\); balance $23,625, below $25,000; margin call $4,375; balance $28,000. Day 3: \\(-\\$1{,}875\\); balance $26,125. Day 4: \\(+0.0060\\times1{,}250{,}000=+\\$7{,}500\\); balance $33,625. Check: cumulative gain \\(0.0010\\times1{,}250{,}000=\\$1{,}250\\); \\(28{,}000+4{,}375+1{,}250=\\$33{,}625\\).\n2.4 Explain why the futures price cannot remain above the spot price during the delivery period. What would a trader do if it did?\nA trader would sell futures, buy the asset in the spot market, and deliver it against the futures, receiving the futures price for an asset bought at the lower spot price. The profit is immediate and riskless. The selling of futures lowers the futures price and the buying of the asset raises the spot price until the two are equal, apart from the costs of delivery."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.exercises Exercises", "body": "2.5 The initial margin on a stock index futures contract with a notional value of $290,000 is $14,500. (a) What percentage of notional value is the margin? (b) If the index falls by 2 percent in a day, what is the loss on a long position as a percentage of the margin deposited?\n(a) \\(14{,}500/290{,}000=5\\) percent. (b) The loss is \\(0.02\\times290{,}000=\\$5{,}800\\), which is \\(5{,}800/14{,}500=40\\) percent of the margin. A margin of 5 percent implies leverage of twenty to one.\n2.6 Open interest in a contract is 42,000 at the start of a day. During the day 9,000 contracts trade: in 4,000 trades both parties open positions, in 3,500 one party opens and one closes, and in 1,500 both close. What is open interest at the end of the day?\n\\(42{,}000+4{,}000-1{,}500=44{,}500\\). The 3,500 trades in which one party opens and the other closes leave open interest unchanged.\n2.7 Describe the default waterfall of a clearinghouse and explain why surviving members' contributions are placed last."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.exercises Exercises", "body": "Losses from a member's default are met, in order, from that member's margin, that member's default fund contribution, a portion of the clearinghouse's own capital, and the default fund contributions of surviving members. Placing the defaulter's resources first ensures that each member bears the cost of its own risk; placing the clearinghouse's capital before the survivors' funds gives the clearinghouse an incentive to set margins prudently; mutualizing the remaining loss among survivors gives every member an interest in admission standards and in the monitoring of others.\n2.8 A firm has sold a product forward to customers at fixed prices for delivery over five years and hedges with three-month futures that it renews each quarter. Identify the principal risk of this arrangement, with reference to the mechanics of this chapter."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:2.exercises", "chapter": 2, "section": "2.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 2, Mechanics of Futures and Forward Markets — 2.exercises Exercises", "body": "The risk is a mismatch in the timing of cash flows. A fall in the price produces losses on the long futures that are settled in cash at once through margin calls, while the corresponding gains on the fixed-price sales are realized only as deliveries occur over five years. The firm must finance the margin calls in the interval. If it cannot, it must close the hedge at a loss and remain exposed on the supply contracts. This was the position of Metallgesellschaft in 1993."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
